2026-09-09,windows,gtk4,P33,ok,ok,window,default,"#115 the on-screen line Still missing: Label(systemImage:) and ColorPicker was false -- Label.swift:143 has a real public init(_:systemImage:) and ColorPicker is at Views/ColorPicker.swift (controls: VStack found, ZZZNotARealType absent). Replaced with a line that names nothing, because this same list was already corrected once on 2026-09-04 from nine names to two and those two went stale in four days. The first draft of the replacement said P46 drives the real views and was ALSO false: P46 drives five of nine and neither of these two (ColorPicker is P48; Label(systemImage:) is P47/P51/P53). The .onAppear log line carried the same false sentence through a build, a strings check and a replay that printed it. Re-ran P33-hide-details.csv after the fix: frame 848x649 UNCHANGED and log details expanded=false, so the coordinate survived -- even though the edit shortened the longest string from 95 to 41 chars, which is exactly what moved P34 66px on 2026-09-04. 848 is not set by the widest Text here"
2026-09-09,windows,gtk4,-,-,-,-,-,"METHOD, from the #115 run, two traps that each give a plausible wrong answer with no error. (1) strings on a Swift binary cannot see a literal of 15 UTF-8 bytes or fewer -- small-string optimisation keeps it out of the binary. Hide details, picked as a POSITIVE CONTROL for a rebuild check, returned 0, which reads as the button being absent. Clean cutoff measured on P33-gtk4.exe: 12 bytes 0, 18 bytes 1. Every control for this check must be 16+ bytes. (2) a leftover P34-gtk4.exe made run.zsh print ZERO BYTES and exit 0 when launching P33, with no log file -- GTK is single-instance by application ID, so ANY leftover P*.exe blocks ANY other. Killing only the app being launched is not enough; sweep every P*.exe and confirm zero. rc=0 plus an empty output file is the signature and both halves look like nothing to report"
2026-09-09,ios,uikit,P55,ok,ok,window,default,buttonstyle isPressed: P55-press-the-styled-button.csv leaves clicks: 2 and pressed draws: 2 (VERIFIED p55-ios-final-20260909-103744.png)
2026-09-09,android,android,P55,ok,ok,window,default,"buttonstyle isPressed: HOLDS rather than taps -- clicks: 2 and pressed draws: 2 (VERIFIED p55-android-final-20260909-105755.png). Two clicks give pressed draws: 1 on the same build, which is the synthesiser rather than the press bridge: a tap presses and releases inside one frame, so whether a layout happens while isPressed is true depends on when the frame lands. AppKit and UIKit happen to give 2 from a plain click"
2026-09-09,windows,gtk4,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"#111 REFUTED as stated. New actions/win/P50-press-inside-popover.csv clicks a button INSIDE the open popover instead of outside it. Two clicks in one run: the anchor at frame 252,711 WORKED (popover alpha shown) while ANOTHER APP held the foreground; press me at frame 180,607 did NOTHING even though hitTarget (WindowFromPoint) reported the POPOVER toplevel, i.e. the cursor was on the popover own button. So the synthesiser is not failing to address the grab: SetCursorPos plus SendInput are PHYSICAL events routed by cursor position and are never posted to a chosen window. The #111 fix as written would change nothing. Foreground ruled out in the surprising direction -- the click that worked was the one made without foreground. Coordinates measured off a DESKTOP capture (p50-popover-desktop-20260909-104720.png), the only kind that contains a popover, since it is a separate toplevel and PrintWindow of the main window never holds it. STILL OPEN: why a physical click on the popover own button produces nothing; needs instrumentation inside GtkBackend event path, not another action file. The new file is EXPECTED UNMET and will pass unedited when fixed"
2026-09-09,windows,gtk4,-,-,-,-,-,"METHOD, from #111. A speculative fix was written and REVERTED the same hour, and the reverting is the point. The GTK popover IS a visible owned toplevel in the replay window dump, and it is invisible to the window walk because two individually correct filters hide it between them (preferringOwners drops owned windows for the DComp GL surface; GW_ENABLEDPOPUP holds ONE slot which DComp has already filled with the main window content surface). Making the walk find it looked like the fix. It is not: the walk choice sets the ORIGIN for origin=frame, so choosing the popover would resolve the light-dismiss click at frame 700,880 against a 291x221 frame and send it off screen. A true mechanism is not a cause. Verify what a change would do to the OTHER files that depend on the same code before believing a diagnosis"
2026-09-09,mac,appkit,P57,ok,-,window,default,"#117 baseline: List does not scroll -- createSelectableListView builds an NSDisabledScrollView with hasVerticalScroller false and the table is laid out at full content height. Window height measured against row count: 10 rows 1280x1958 px, 50 rows 1280x9306, 100 rows 640x9253, and at 200 and 400 the window capture fails and falls back to the whole screen. That is the number virtualisation has to change"
2026-09-09,android,android,P9,ok,ok,window,default,"Q4 closed: the clipping is reachable, not lost. At rest P9 shows '9: text and field sizing' and 'ext field' -- the left edge is off screen -- and one scroll left brings back 'P9:' and 'Text field' whole (p9-android-final-20260909-122702.png)"
2026-09-09,android,android,P6,ok,ok,window,default,"Q4 closed on the widest case (889L 892R): at rest the title reads 'stream player', the tail of 'P6: Zstd stream player'; scrolling left brings back 'P6: Zst...' and 'P6-actio...' (p6-android-final-20260909-123128.png)"
2026-09-09,-,-,-,-,-,-,-,"Q4 VERDICT on all 16 right-clipped scenarios: not a backend defect. The apps are wider than a phone, and AndroidRootScrollHost.onLayout deliberately starts at Stage.originOffset rather than 0 -- RootScrollHost.kt:170 records why: starting at zero showed the blank margin left of the content and broke 46 action files at once, fifteen apps reporting 'replayed and changed no pixel'. Overflow stays reachable in both directions, which is what these two runs check"
2026-09-09,mac,appkit,P57,ok,-,window,default,"#117 phase 2: the window height stops growing. 10/50/100/200/400 rows all give 1280x1958 px, where before it was 1958, 9306, 9253 and uncapturable past 200. The list scrolls itself now -- rows 10-25 visible at 400 rows with a scroller (p57-macos-final-20260909-124522.png). NOT virtualisation: every row is still built and laid out, only the reported height changed"
2026-09-09,ios,uikit,P57,ok,-,window,default,"#117 phase 4a on iOS. The AppKit metric (does the window stop growing) cannot fail here -- the window is the screen -- and two captures at 50 and 400 rows were both 2556 px and would have been with the change deleted. Measured in-process instead, one binary, only -rows varying: 10 rows frame.h 340 content.h 440; 50 rows 382/2200; 400 rows 382/17600; 2000 rows 382/88000. Content linear at 44 pt a row, frame constant = the viewport. NOT virtualisation: every row is still built. Also: the first four readings came from a stale install, P57.app is dev.swiftcrossui.testapp.P57 and the launches used ...debugTarget left by test_ios.zsh -- it started and screenshotted every time"
2026-09-09,android,android,P57,ok,-,window,default,"#117 phase 4a on Android. Emulator 1080x2400 density 2.625, same APK, only -rows varying: layoutParams.height is 1181 px (450 pt) at 50, 400 and 2000 rows -- constant across fortyfold. Screenshot at 2000 rows shows rows 0..12, so a viewport and not a list clipped to nothing. Before-value not measured separately on this backend: without the conformance setViewportHeight is never called, so the probe has nothing to print; the old height is List.swift sum over rows, the expression AppKit pinned"
2026-09-09,mac,appkit,P57,ok,-,window,default,"#117 phase 3 baseline, and a correction to the phase 3 criterion. At 10000 rows the app opens, window 640x979 (same as 400 and 2000) and rows 1..25 render -- so the planned test (does it still open at 10000) already passed before the work it gated. Two timings cannot tell the two apart either: P57 self-reports firstRender=0.002s at 400 rows because onAppear precedes row construction, and process-start to RENDER COMPLETE is 0.10s at both 400 and 10000 (interleaved, minimum of 4). Resident memory is what grows: 114 MB at 400 rows vs 423 MB at 10000 (interleaved, minimum of 3), about 32 KB a row and linear. New criterion: RSS stops growing with the row count. Also P57 now writes p57-debug-events.log per the SCUI_DEBUG_EVENTS_DIR contract -- it previously only printed, which is what let an earlier sweep read a missing file as RENDERED everywhere"
2026-09-09,mac,appkit,P58,ok,ok,window,"P58-left-to-L30.csv, P58-right-to-R45.csv","Review item 4 closed on AppKit (VERIFIED, driven). New app: ONE ScrollViewReader over TWO ScrollViews, the shape the per-container registry fix addressed and that nothing in the tree drove -- P56 has one scroll view, and with one the broken and fixed code do the same thing. Left press: left column shows L11..L30 while the right still shows R0..R19 (p58-macos-final-20260909-151052.png). Right press: right column shows R26..R45 while the left is back at L0..L19 (p58-macos-final-20260909-151110.png). Both directions are needed -- one alone cannot tell per-container keying from every scroll landing on the same container. The column that does NOT move is half the reading; a capture of one column reads the same under both"
2026-09-09,android,android,P58,ok,ok,window,"P58-left-to-L30.csv (android), P58-right-to-R45.csv (android)","Review item 4 closed on Android too (VERIFIED, driven). The registry keying is shared Swift but scrollContainer is not -- Android decides container membership by walking the child parent chain where AppKit uses isDescendant(of:), so an AppKit-only test leaves that path unexecuted. Left press: left column L15..L30, right column still R0..R15 (p58-android-final-20260909-152949.png). Right press: right column R30..R45, left column back at L0..L15 (p58-android-final-20260909-153009.png). Two traps hit on the way, both silent: test_android.zsh built the APK without SCUI_DEBUG so the replay was compiled out -- it exited 0 and captured an untouched app, now fixed in the script; and the Android synthesiser replays at the platform scale, so coordinates are POINTS, and device pixels (175,481) put the click at (459,1263) while still reporting replayed 3 actions"
2026-09-09,mac,appkit,P58,ok,ok,window,"P58-left-to-L30.csv, P58-right-to-R45.csv","Re-verified after narrowing both columns from 300 to 150 (flow.md 3e-1). The width was changed because two 300-wide columns are 616 points across, wider than a 1080 px phone at density 2.625: the first Android capture showed the right column alone with the left column and the left button off screen. Left press: left column L11..L30, right still R0..R19 (p58-macos-final-20260909-151806.png). Narrowing did not put AppKit overlay scroll bar back on the digits -- labels sit at the left of the frame, the bar at the right"
2026-09-09,mac,appkit,P59,ok,ok,window,P59-two-windows-one-typed.csv,"SceneStorage landed and driven (VERIFIED). New wrapper: @SceneStorage is scoped to the window id WindowReference already hands to createWindow(withDefaultSize:id:), stored through the existing AppStorageProvider under scene.<id>.<key> -- no new backend requirement and no second persistence path. After three appends in window A and then opening window B, the app UserDefaults domain holds exactly: draft=AAA (app-wide), scene.TupleView1<HotReloadableView>-0.draft=AAA (window A), and NO scene.p59-second.draft at all. Window B renders scene draft (empty) beside app draft AAA. The absent third key is the assertion: scoping is a different key, not a different value under one key. Two traps on the way -- the first action file opened the second window BEFORE typing, so all three clicks landed in window B (probe: three SETs, all sceneID=p59-second) and the capture of window A read as a failure while being correct; and the harness runs the BUNDLED app (domain dev.swiftcrossui.testapp.p59) while a direct binary run uses domain P59, so a stale domain made an earlier reading look like leakage"
2026-09-09,mac,appkit,P60,ok,ok,window,P60-open-settings.csv,"Settings scene landed and driven (VERIFIED). New scene: Settings { } plus environment openSettings. How it appears is the backend answer, not the app one -- AppKit opens a separate window titled Settings, and the capture shows it with its own title bar carrying count 0 and a +1 button (screencapture of the desktop, both windows visible). The +1 half is not driven on this backend and the file says why: no action-file origin addresses a SECOND window; frame and client both resolve against the driven window and popover is Windows-only. That half is driven on iOS where settings is a sheet"
2026-09-09,ios,uikit,P60,ok,-,window,default,"Settings scene on the single-window path (VERIFIED by launch argument, not by an action file). UIKit reports supportsMultipleWindows=false, so settings is presented as a SHEET over the one window -- capture shows the sheet with P60 settings, count 0 and +1. Driven with --auto-settings, which calls openSettings() from the root view onAppear; the action-file tap at the button is NOT landing and is recorded as unresolved rather than claimed. Two real defects found by this path, both silent: openSettings() called before the sheet host appeared warned and did nothing (a root onAppear fires before the host one), now remembered as a pending request; and honouring that request inline swallowed the state flip because it ran inside the committing update, so it is deferred to the next runloop turn. Both were measured -- an immediate call did nothing while the identical call two seconds later presented the sheet"
2026-09-09,ios,uikit,P60,ok,ok,window,P60-open-settings.csv (ios),"Settings-as-sheet now DRIVEN BY THE ACTION FILE, replacing the earlier launch-argument workaround. The tap was missing the button and three things had to be separated to know that: the app log (openSettings never called, so the tap missed rather than being ignored), a new diagnostic in the XCUITest runner (window frame 0,0,393,852 and normalized 0.153/0.117 -- the runner was never wrong), and a measurement of the button itself off a cropped pixel band (row ~653 of 1179x2556 at scale 3 = point 218, where the earlier y=100 had been eyeballed from a 700px preview). With (55,218) the log reads openSettings() called then settings view appeared, and the capture shows the sheet. Also fixed on the way: P60 wrote its debug log to the working directory, which on iOS is a read-only / -- the write failed silently and looked like the app never reaching the line"
2026-09-09,mac,appkit,P60,ok,ok,window,P60-open-settings.csv,"Second window now DRIVEN, closing the gap this row previously recorded as impossible. New `focus` verb names a window by exact title in a tenth column, `target`; files without it parse unchanged. The action file opens settings, focuses it, presses +1 INSIDE it, focuses back, and the capture of the main window reads count: 1 -- so the settings view shares the app state rather than being a copy. What actually blocked it was not the verb: AppKitSynthesiser never overrode currentWindowIdentity(), whose default returns 0, and 0 is the value replay treats as cannot-tell -- so geometry was never re-measured and coordinates after a focus still resolved against the window measured before the replay began. With windowNumber returned, the replay reports target window changed and re-measures to frame=(1470,421). Two coordinate conversions were wrong before that: this display is 1x, so a desktop capture pixel IS a point, and halving by an assumed 2x put the click 132 points above the button"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P50,ok,ok,window,P50-show-title-b.csv,"navigationTitle CHANGE now reaches the window (user-reported defect, fixed). Before: pressing Show title B changed the app body -- the green box read TITLE B -- while the title bar still read TITLE A in the same screenshot. Cause measured with a probe: window chrome is applied only during a WINDOW layout pass, and ViewGraphNode.bottomUpUpdate commits a state change without telling anything above it when the size is unchanged, so the preference was recomputed and never read (three probe firings, all at launch, all pref=TITLE A). Fix: a new EnvironmentValues.onWindowChromeChange, raised by a node only when the window-facing preference signature (title plus toolbar labels) actually differs, and handled by WindowReference with the same update the resize handler runs. Also fixed here: test_P50.zsh had TEST_TITLE set to the app description, which matches no window, so every capture silently fell back to the whole desktop -- the substring that matches both title states is P50 TITLE"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P50,ok,ok,window,P50-open-first-panel.csv,"Second user-reported symptom LOCATED, not yet fixed. The main window does NOT shift when a panel opens -- two desktop captures cropped to the same window rectangle put every line in the same place. What moves is the PANEL itself: its text and both buttons are drawn wider than the green presentationBackground and hang over both edges, so the leading padding disappears and the first characters are clipped. Measured with a probe: updatePopover is given 284x154, the content view is 284x154 and its subview is 284x154 -- the sizes agree, and the text inside simply draws wider than the width the layout computed for it. So the fault is in what the content measures as, not in how AppKit places it. Also seen: onDismiss logged popover alpha dismissed TWICE for one light dismissal. Driving the panel from an action file is not possible on this backend -- the synthesiser posts into the main window, so a click at the panel dismisses it, which is what origin=popover exists for on Windows"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P50,ok,ok,window,P50-open-first-panel.csv,"onDismiss fired TWICE for one dismissal -- fixed. A probe showed the SAME popover object reaching popoverDidClose twice (identical ObjectIdentifier), so it was not two popovers. Cause is documented AppKit behaviour: NSPopover automatically registers a delegate that implements a notification-shaped method as an observer of the matching notification, so the method arrives once by delegate dispatch and once from the notification centre. The consequence is not cosmetic -- PopoverModifier.handleDismiss runs the application closure before tearing state down, so an app that saves a draft on dismissal did it twice. Fixed with a per-presentation flag reset in presentPopover; P50 now logs one dismissal where it logged two"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P50,ok,ok,window,P50-open-first-panel.csv,"CORRECTION to the row above, and the reported shift does not reproduce. Two earlier claims were wrong and both came from a crop whose own boundary cut the image: the panel content is NOT clipped and does NOT overflow its background -- with margin around the crop the text is flush to the edge because P50Panel asks for no padding, and the green background contains it. Measured numerically instead of by eye, three desktop captures cropped to the same window rect: opening the panel changes only the popover region (73,455)-(395,654) and masking that rect leaves getbbox() = None, so not one pixel of the main window moves; and with the panel open, driving the counter 0 to 1 changes only (160,579)-(278,707), the label and the count line. No shift in either. The counter is driven by a debug-only --auto-press argument because an action file cannot press a button inside an AppKit popover: the synthesiser posts into the main window, so the click light-dismisses the panel instead"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P32,ok,ok,window,P32-click-toggle.csv,"Toggle had NO visible on state on AppKit (user-reported, fixed). NSCustomToggleButton stored onStateBezelColor and setState painted bezelColor = state ? onStateBezelColor : nil -- and onStateBezelColor comes from environment.toggleColor, which is nil for any app that does not set one. So the on state painted nothing. It now falls back to .controlAccentColor, the colour every other control on the platform uses to say on. Verified: the toggle draws in the accent colour and the new readout reads ON. Two things this app was missing that hid it: P32 never displayed its own toggle state, so a toggle that never changed and a toggle that changed invisibly produced identical captures; and the harness with --no-showtime captured BEFORE the replay finished, which read as a click that did nothing -- --showtime 5 shows the change at (16,248)-(122,324). An earlier claim that TEST_TARGET=both drops --actionfile is WITHDRAWN: re-tested, the flag is honoured without a platform flag"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P44,ok,-,window,default,"Layout warning was a false positive and is fixed. P44 logged vertical stack ran out of space: 9 children were offered 643 and took 643, firstStarvedChild=Spacer -- offered and took are EQUAL, so nothing ran out. The report fired on share == 0 alone, and a Spacer with no slack correctly takes zero. It now also requires the stack to have overflowed, which is what its own message claims. The case it was written for is unaffected: four columns offered 350 while needing 528, the fourth Text offered zero and wrapping one character per line. Pinned by Tests/SwiftCrossUITests/StackOverflowReportTests.swift, and the test was shown to fail with the guard removed rather than only shown to pass"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P28,ok,ok,window,P28-hit-testing.csv,"Reported ~1 second from click to the counter updating; MEASURED and it does not reproduce on the synthesised path. Both clocks aligned on the same monotonic base -- the app logs click at uptime and the observer samples CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW -- rather than estimating from the action files sleeps, which was off by half a second on the first attempt. Click to body: 0-2 ms. Click to PIXELS: 69 ms at 52 ms sampling, so one or two frames. Also measured: one click runs the body FIVE times, each fast, which may share a cause with the n^1.5 layout cost item. Two paths remain unmeasured and are the likely difference from the report: a real mouse event goes through the full AppKit queue rather than being posted to the window, and the first click after launch is not warm. P28 gained a click-latency line, so the action files y moved from 271 to 285"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P61,ok,ok,window,P61-drag-then-code.csv,"New app for Slider.onEditingChanged (#126), and it carries its own control. A drag reports began 1 / ended 1; pressing move-it-from-code then changes the value and adds NEITHER -- the readout ends at began 1, ended 1, values seen 2, editing no. That second half is the assertion: a backend firing onEditingChanged from its VALUE handler would look correct for every drag and wrong only there. HONEST LIMIT: the synthesised drag produced one value, so many-values-one-edit is shown by two values rather than by a long drag -- AppKit tracks a slider in its own modal loop and posted move events do not feed it. Two API shapes found while writing this, both loud compile errors rather than silent: the modifier has to be on the public Slider rather than the internal SliderControl, and it has to come before any modifier that erases the type"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P33,ok,-,window,default,"Inventory done (user reported lots of features missing). P33 renders correctly and its own text is accurate: it holds three hand-built approximations -- Stepper, DisclosureGroup, LabeledContent -- and says the real views are in P46. Re-derived with declaration-shaped greps plus a control: of sixteen names checked (Stepper, DisclosureGroup, LabeledContent, Gauge, ControlGroup, GroupBox, ColorPicker, LazyVGrid, LazyHGrid, ScrollViewReader, Table, Form, TabView, NavigationSplitView, Menu, ProgressView) only LazyHGrid is absent, which is #118 and already queued; ZZZNotARealType correctly reported absent, so the method could still find something missing. Nothing else on P33 list is missing"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P34,ok,-,window,default,"Inventory done, and a real defect found while doing it. P34 own text is accurate -- LazyVStack and LazyHStack exist but build every child up front, LazyHGrid is absent. The defect is in the row list: rows whose number has TWO digits are CUT OFF at the right. Measured on the capture: row 0 text ends at x=316 while rows 10 and 18 both stop at x=319, the container edge, mid-character. So the scrolling container is narrower than its widest child, which is the same family as the P50 popover finding -- something is measured narrower than it draws. LayoutSystem:239 does take the max of children on the perpendicular axis, so the fault is not that rule. Not yet located; queued"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P62,ok,ok,window,P62-two-documents.csv,"DocumentGroup landed and driven. New: FileDocument (Data in, Data out, throwing both ways), DocumentGroup scene with one window per document, environment newDocument and openDocument actions, and ContentType.plainText. Driven: typing twice in the first document takes it to AA (log), then new document opens a second window that reads (empty) (capture) -- if every window shared one document the new one would read AA. HONEST LIMITS, all stated in the scene own documentation rather than left to be found: no autosave, no versions, no unsaved-changes prompt on close, no reopening last session. And two about this run: the second window opens exactly on top of the first, both being untitled, so ONE capture cannot show both -- the assertion is the log line plus the capture; and P62 per-window label is unreliable because a @State initialised by a side-effecting counter runs on every view construction, so the second window is labelled 6 rather than 2. That is the test app fault, not the scenes"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P25,ok,-,window,default,"Question answered by reading the code, and the answer is that P25 already receives multiple files. DropPayload.urls parses every entry of an RFC 2483 text/uri-list, firstURL exists only as a convenience for the single-file case, and P25 already displays count = items.count -- so a multi-file drop shows a count above one. No API is needed to control single versus multiple for a DROP: a drop delivers whatever the source provides. NOT DRIVEN: a drag from Finder cannot be synthesised by the action-file format, so this is code-level evidence rather than a run. The single-versus-multiple choice DOES exist for the open DIALOG, which was genuinely single-only -- see the chooseFiles action added the same day"
2026-09-10,windows,winui,P54,ok,ok,window,default,"refreshable: DRIVEN, not just compiled. The header said Written on macOS and NOT verified and 66725261 said COMPILE-VERIFIED ONLY; there was no windows,winui,P54 row in this file at all. actions/win/P54-press-the-refresh.csv presses the floating Refresh button at frame (37,45) twice: refreshes: 2 and rows up to: 10, matching the AppKit and UIKit runs of 2026-09-09 exactly. Both open questions in the header resolve: a Grid hosts the button fine and it does not need its own row. Two presses rather than one, because one cannot tell a working counter from one stuck at 1 (VERIFIED P54-20260910-141850.png)"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P52,ok,ok,none,unrecorded,"#113: four arms x five sizes (12..192 cells). press cost per cell is FLAT (220/166/63 us for primitive/custom/text) -> LINEAR, exponent n^0.95..0.96, not n^1.5. Same exponent on all three arms, so growth is in the stack layout; Button adds a constant ~157us per cell (3.5x a Text of the same shape). At 48 cells one press is 10.5ms min / 20.7ms median, not the 0.3s reference. Fixed on the way: four per-arm arrays were count:3 and a fourth arm killed the process before step one."
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P34,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"two-digit rows no longer clipped: text is now measured with the NSTextField that draws it (cell.cellSize) instead of NSString.boundingRect, which under-reports by ~4pt on every string tried. Before: rows 0-9 ended at x=316 and every row from 10 up stopped dead at x=319 mid-glyph. After: row ends vary 312..320 by their own width."
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P28,ok,ok,n/a,unrecorded,"real mouse path MEASURED, closing the two paths 577d1333 left open. CGEvent.post(.cghidEventTap) delivers once AXIsProcessTrusted() is true (it was false when the synthesiser header was written, which is where -cannot be synthesised- came from). Click to body: 16.0ms on the first click after launch with no warm-up, 2.9-9.9ms warmed, three runs. Nothing near the reported one second."
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P34,ok,n/a,n/a,unrecorded,"accessibility: every AXButton in P17, P28 and P34 had an empty title AND description before this - the label went to a sibling text field and the NSCustomButton override that was meant to supply it is never consulted, because a plain NSView is transparent in the accessibility tree. The label now reaches the inner NSButton. After: desc=Show +100 / Scroll to row 50 / Clickable button underneath / Shortest."
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P51,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#120 Grid: columns are now shared across rows and gridCellColumns spans. Measured on the capture: C2 is 106..155 and C3 is 166..215 in ALL THREE rows (before, each row started C2 wherever its own C1 ended); the span row draws 16..215, which is column 1 left edge to column 3 right edge. Five unit tests, and the span-distribution one was proved to fail with the widening disabled."
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P48,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#118 LazyHGrid driven: section 5 fills two fixed 34pt rows top-to-bottom then moves right (1/2, 3/4, 5/6), lane pitch measured 42 = 34 + 8. Section 6 aligns three 40pt rows top/center/bottom: cell tops measured 52px apart where the pitch is 48, the extra 4 being (40-32)/2, so the three lanes really do differ."
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P63,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#127 frame(in:) driven and checked against pixels. Reader reports local (0,0), global (92,287), named box (24,31). Measured on the capture: the marker square is x=92..103 and its top is 287 below the content edge, so global matches exactly; the light box corner measures (68,256) and global minus named is (68,256), so the named space matches exactly too. Origin comes from a new backend requirement, WidgetGeometry."
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P64,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#28 frame clock DRIVEN, not just compiled: 121 ticks in 2.00s = 60.0 Hz, gap median 16.7ms (one frame at 60Hz), min 7.9 max 25.4. The requirement is BackendFeatures.FrameClocks; AppKit uses NSView.displayLink on macOS 14+ and a 60Hz timer below it."
2026-09-10,ios,uikit,P64,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#28 frame clock on CADisplayLink in .common mode: 122 ticks in 2.03s = 59.7 Hz, gap min 16.7 median 16.7 max 27.7ms. Ran on the simulator, not only compiled."
2026-09-10,windows,gtk4,P51,ok,ok,window,default,"#120 VERIFIED on Win-gtk4 by pixel analysis of a window capture, not by eye -- my own first read of the same image was wrong. Section 2 vertical border strokes per row: r1 [30,70,113,153,165,205], r2 identical, r3 [30,101,113,153,165,205], r4 [30,205]. So C2=113..153 and C3=165..205 are IDENTICAL across all three rows, widening R3C1 does not push C2, and gridCellColumns(3) spans C1-left to C3-right. Same structure as the Mac numbers (C2 106..155, C3 166..215); absolute values differ by window width"
2026-09-10,windows,winui,P51,ok,ok,window,default,"#120 VERIFIED on Win-WinUI, same method. r1 [24,68,117,161,173,217], r2 identical, r3 [24,105,117,161,173,217], r4 [24,217]. C2 and C3 identical across rows, span reaches C3 right edge. Structurally identical to gtk4 and to AppKit"
2026-09-10,windows,gtk4,P48,ok,ok,window,default,"#118 VERIFIED on Win-gtk4. Section 5 LazyHGrid lane pitch 42 with cell height 34 -- matches the Mac 42=34+8 exactly. Section 6 alignment staircase: cell tops 51 apart, twice. Mac measured 52; all-centred would be 48, so the staircase is present and the 1px is a metrics difference (Pango vs CoreText), not a missing alignment"
2026-09-10,windows,winui,P48,ok,ok,window,default,"#118 VERIFIED on Win-WinUI. Section 5 lane pitch 42, same as both others. Section 6 staircase 52 and 52 -- IDENTICAL to the AppKit numbers. So the 1px gtk4 difference is gtk4-only"
2026-09-10,windows,gtk4,P64,ok,fail,window,default,"#28 frame clock BUILDS and starts but ticks ONCE. P64 reports TICKS 1 -- too few to measure a rate, three seconds after starting; AppKit gets 121 in 2.00s and UIKit 122 in 2.03s. Two obvious causes ruled out: the tick callback DOES return 1 (G_SOURCE_CONTINUE), and P64 just appends to a timestamps array with no logic in the way, so the handler is genuinely called once. The file own named risk -- frameClockWidget finding no window -- is also ruled out, since a missing widget would give zero ticks rather than one. Remaining candidate: a GtkWindow may not be a widget that keeps producing frames on its own. Needs one more round"
2026-09-10,windows,winui,P64,ok,fail,window,default,"#28 frame clock RUNS but at the wrong rate: TICKS 262 in 1.86s -> 140.4 Hz, gap min 0.0ms median 0.0ms max 62.0ms. AppKit gets 60.0 and UIKit 60.1. A median gap of ZERO means many ticks share one timestamp, so either CompositionTarget.rendering fires more than once per composed frame, or ProcessInfo.systemUptime is not the resolution this needs on Windows. 140.4/60 is about 2.3 events per frame, which fits the first. Also needed two build fixes before it ran at all: addHandler returns EventCleanup not EventRegistrationToken (so unsubscribing is dispose(), not removeHandler), and CompositionTarget.rendering needs @preconcurrency import under Swift 6"
2026-09-10,mac,appkit,P65,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#32 drag gesture DRIVEN with a real CGEvent drag and checked against pixels: panel at screen (80,252), drag posted (150,290)->(230,320), app reported start (70,38) location (150,68) translation (80,30) - all three exact. Magnify and rotate compile and are not driven: no synthesiser here produces a trackpad gesture."
2026-09-11,windows,gtk4,P34,ok,ok,window,default,"text measure-vs-draw check (the AppKit 4pt bug shape): NOT present. Pixel analysis of a window capture -- single-digit rows end at x=335 width 144, double-digit rows end at x=342 width 151. They differ by 7px, one digit, so the wider rows really are wider rather than all stopping at one x. Structural reason: GtkBackend measures with Pango(for: widget) where widget IS the CustomLabel being drawn, same font context and the same PANGO_WRAP_WORD_CHAR the label uses"
2026-09-11,windows,winui,P34,ok,ok,window,default,"text measure-vs-draw check: NOT present, and the widths match gtk4 exactly -- single-digit 144, double-digit 151, the same 7px step. WinUIBackend measures by calling widget.measure() then reading widget.desiredSize on the SAME element it draws. Noted while reading it: sizeCorrection(for:) is a table of hardcoded pixel adjustments for elements whose padding reads zero before first render, with a comment saying the value came from someone else VM. That is the same family of bug (measure path and draw path disagreeing) and is worth its own check"
2026-09-11,windows,gtk4,accessibility,n/a,fail,none,default,"GTK4 on Windows has the accessibility API and NO bridge to any assistive technology, so the question -what does each button say to a screen reader- has no answer on this backend: nothing reads the tree at all. Evidence with controls: C:/gtk4/lib and /bin contain ZERO atk, at-spi or a11y libraries against 67 dlls present; gtk-4-1.dll contains 130 occurrences of gtk_accessible and 0 of UiaRaiseAutomationEvent, IRawElementProviderSimple, UIAutomationCore or uiautomationcore, with gtk_widget_grab_focus at 2 as a positive control. So gtk_accessible_update_property compiles, runs and reaches nobody. This is a platform fact rather than a defect in this project, and it means a UIA dump tool for gtk4 would have nothing to dump"
2026-09-11,mac,appkit,P66,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#28 withAnimation DRIVEN: a linear 0.5s tween of a Double from 0 to 200 produced 31 distinct values, monotonic, first five 0/7.3/13.9/20.6/27.2 (about 6.7 per frame at 60Hz) and last exactly 200.0. A Bool assigned in the same withAnimation block took exactly 1 assignment, because Bool does not conform to AnimatableValue. Two captures 3s apart during a 6s return trip show the marker at x=108 and x=60."
2026-09-11,windows,winui,accessibility,n/a,unchecked,none,default,"No automation name is ever SET, on either layer: WinUIBackend has 0 occurrences of AutomationProperties and 0 of AutomationPeer (control: buttonStyle 7), and SwiftCrossUI has 0 files mentioning accessibilityLabel (control: EnvironmentValues in 176). So whether a button has a usable name rests entirely on UIA deriving one from Button.Content -- which is a SwiftCrossUI view wrapper, not a bare string, and deriving a name from a wrapper is precisely the mechanism that failed on AppKit. CANNOT BE CHECKED FROM SWIFT: the whole of Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation.Peers.swift (1204 lines) exposes exactly one public class, AutomationPeer, with no fromElement and no FrameworkElementAutomationPeer, so there is no way to ask an element for its peer. Needs inspect.exe (present at Windows Kits 10.0.22621.0, but GUI-only) or a UIA COM tool"
2026-09-11,mac,appkit,P67,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#123 accessibility names, four button shapes: ax_dump reports desc=plain label / padded label / two / (empty for the image-only one). The padded and two-text shapes are the ones the old index-based lookup could not name."
2026-09-11,ios,uikit,P67,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#123 accessibility names on the simulator: the app walks its own UIKit tree and shows names: plain label / padded label / two / (empty). Identical to AppKit. UIKit previously read subviews[1] as a WrapperWidget<TextView>, which named only the plainest shape."
2026-09-11,mac,appkit,P66,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"A3 Color animation: the same 0.5s tween that moved a Double from 0 to 200 in 29 steps also produced 29 DISTINCT colours. A .system colour assigns at once instead, which is what a non-conforming type does; holding the old colour and snapping at the end was the alternative and reads as a bug."
2026-09-11,mac,appkit,P50,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"A6 origin=popover now works on AppKit: P50-panel-press-me.csv opens the panel and presses the button INSIDE it, and P50 logs -popover counter 1-. Two things had to change: targetWindow no longer returns a popover (a popover takes key, so every non-popover coordinate was resolving against it), and events for origin=popover are posted to the popovers own window (posting to the window behind light-dismisses it)."
2026-09-11,mac,appkit,P57,ok,n/a,n/a,unrecorded,"#117 phase 3 baseline re-measured: 400 rows 113 MB RSS, 10000 rows 423 MB, about 31 KB per row. The cost is one AnyViewGraphNode per row in the framework, not backend widgets - NSTableView recycles cells already."
2026-09-11,mac,appkit,P68,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"A7: AppKit now implements GraphicsAdapters through Metal. -GPU 0 reports -already using software rendering-, -GPU 1 prints nothing (the default returns early), -GPU 2 reports -using Apple M4 (fell back: no external adapter is attached)- with the fallback reason beside the outcome. One adapter listed: Apple M4, not removable, not low power."
2026-09-11,mac,appkit,P50,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"popover content was pinned to the shells bottom-left: updatePopover set the content view controllers view frame to origin .zero, which overrode AppKits own placement. Shell 314x180 against content 288x154, so 26 points per axis that AppKit would have split evenly were pushed entirely to the top and right edges - the panel hung off the left and white leaked on the right. Reported by the user. Fixed by not repositioning that view and letting the child autoresize with it."
